{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788180346,"nonce":"z8kx8EuqSCaBDwuGUvdgDtJu66Q=","message":"CLOSED — acceptance verified personally on the BORGES bench, orchestrator\nsession 6. Merged to main as `ccb8d67` (3-way), DLL at `2558b08`.\nSix verbs: designate (26 types) / forbid / unforbid / flick / zone / area.\n\nACCEPTANCE BULLET 1 — chop-all-in-rect, jobs actually taken\n\n    designate {type:chop, rect:[89,86,20,26], dry_run:true}\n      -\u003e designator Designator_PlantsHarvestWood\n         gate      RimWorld/Designator_PlantsHarvestWood.CanDesignateThing\n         accepted  25   targeted 520   designations_before == _now == 0\n         action.journal_seq null, provenance \"not applicable — dry_run mutated nothing\"\n    designate {type:chop, rect:[89,86,20,26]}\n      -\u003e accepted 25, designations 0 -\u003e 25, crop legend \"*\" = HarvestPlant,\n         action.journal_seq 17\n    advance {ticks:60000}  -\u003e reason \"ticks\", tick 1307 -\u003e 61307, 0 events\n    things {def:\"WoodLog\"}  -\u003e  700 (11 stacks)  BEFORE\n                            -\u003e 1077 (21 stacks)  AFTER      (+377)\n    designations remaining  -\u003e  25 -\u003e 14\n\n25 mature trees from ONE envelope over 520 cells — the plural contract. Two\nindependent readings agree that pawns took the jobs: wood up 377 across 10 new\nstacks, and 11 designations consumed off the list.\n\nACCEPTANCE BULLET 2 — growing zone and a meds-only stockpile\n\n    zone {op:add, kind:growing, rect:[114,114,5,5]}   -\u003e REFUSED bad-args,\n      \"zone add growing needs 'plant' (… Zone_Growing.PlantDefToGrow assigns\n       Potato or Toxipotato on first read). Pass allow_unset_plant:true …\"\n    zone {op:add, kind:growing, …, plant:\"Plant_Rice\"}\n      -\u003e gate Designator_ZoneAdd_Growing.CanDesignateCell (-\u003e IsZoneableCell)\n         accepted 25, plant_configured true, plant_source \"backing-field\"\n    advance {ticks:120000}   (2 game days, tick 61307 -\u003e 181307, reason \"ticks\")\n    zones {kind:growing} -\u003e planted 25/25, wrong_plant 0, empty_cells 0,\n                            growth_avg_pct 17 (min 15, max 22)\n\n`plant_source:\"backing-field\"` is the WorldSafe guard proving the read did not go\nthrough the scribing `PlantDefToGrow` getter.\n\nThe stockpile was proven with VANILLA HAULING AS THE INDEPENDENT ACTOR, per the\narrange-act-read lesson. My first attempt sited it on the starting loot pile and\nI threw that away — reading a zone I had contaminated proves nothing. Re-sited on\nempty ground at [94,114], 36 cells, filter meds, priority Preferred; then 20\nMedicineHerbal spawned at [100,112] and 75 Steel at [101,113], both OUTSIDE:\n\n    stockpile contents BEFORE  -\u003e  Steel x33  (a pre-existing squatter) + plants\n    stockpile contents AFTER   -\u003e  Steel x33  UNCHANGED, MedicineHerbal x50\n    things {def:\"MedicineHerbal\"} -\u003e 50, 2 stacks, ALL of it at [94,114]\n\nEvery piece of herbal medicine on the map converged into the zone; not one unit of\nthe 75 steel 13 cells away was hauled in. The steel already inside predates the\nzone and has nowhere else to go (this map has one stockpile) — vanilla does not\neject, so that is correct behaviour, not a filter failure. Stated plainly because\n\"received exactly meds\" is about what was ACCEPTED, not about what was lying on\nthe ground first.\n\nACCEPTANCE BULLET 3 — rejects report real reasons\n\n    mine on soil        -\u003e \"Must designate impassable mineable rocks.\"\n    chop, empty cells   -\u003e \"Must designate wood-yielding, sufficiently-grown plants.\"\n    forbid, empty cells -\u003e \"Must designate unforbidden items.\"\n    zone priority Unstored -\u003e REFUSED, \"not selectable (RimWorld/ITab_Storage\n                              .FillTab skips it); use Low|Normal|Preferred|…\"\n    zone filter {category,hp} -\u003e REFUSED, naming spec 3.6 and IStoreSettingsParent\n\nAll verbatim from the game's own Keyed strings. ONE HONEST NUANCE: `tame` on a\ncolonist returns `why:\"not-designatable\"` with `reason:null`. I checked —\n`Designator_Tame.CanDesignateThing` returns a bare `false` for that case and only\nsupplies a string for Scaria, so null is the game having nothing to say, not the\nverb losing it. Blockers.cs's rule (never a phrase of ours dressed up as the\ngame's) is being followed correctly.\n\nFOG — Dorian's session-3 resolution, and it is OUR gate\n\n`Designator_Mine.CanDesignateCell` returns TRUE for a fogged cell; the game's\ndesignators disagree with each other about fog, so 3.2 puts one uniform gate in\nfront of all of them. Verified on genuinely fogged cells at [110,135]:\n\n    designate chop -\u003e \"fogged / unexplored\"   forbid -\u003e \"fogged / unexplored\"\n\nSame rejection shape from two different verbs.\n\nTHE FLICK TRAP — the most important thing in this spec\n\n`FlickUtility.UpdateFlickDesignation` ends in\n`TutorUtility.DoModalDialogIfNotKnown(SwitchFlickingDesignation)` -\u003e\n`new Dialog_MessageBox`, which sets `forcePause`. Per 1.7 that halts every later\n`advance` at 0 ticks with nothing able to clear it until 3.5 ships. The worker\nre-implemented the designation half line-for-line and dropped only that call.\n\nTHE COUNTERFACTUAL IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. `Knowledge.xml` on this bench has\n`SwitchFlickingDesignation` at 0, and no concept in the file exceeds 0 — nothing\nhas ever been demonstrated on this save. `IsComplete` is `\u003e 0.999f`, so vanilla\nWOULD have fired the modal on the colony's first power switch. Live:\n\n    flick {things:[43443], on:false}\n      -\u003e wants_on false, switch_is_on TRUE, flick_designated true\n    interactions -\u003e force_pause {count:0, windows:[]}\n\nBoth halves right: the designation echoed rather than the state (a verb claiming\n\"power off\" would be lying — a colonist must walk over and do the job), and no\nmodal.\n\nSTANDING INVARIANTS\n\n    red_error events         0   (across both bench sessions)\n    action journal lines     7   one per mutating call, in causal order\n    cheat / fog_exempt       absent on every one — an action is not a cheat\n    dry-runs and refusals    wrote NO action line\n    force-pausing windows    none at any point\n\nORCHESTRATOR FIX DURING ACCEPTANCE (`037c5df`)\n\n`zone add stockpile --rect … --filter meds` — THIS SPEC'S OWN BULLET 2 — was\nimpossible: `DesignateEngine.Resolve` consumed `filter` as one of its four\nmutually-exclusive target-set keys, so the call died on \"rect, cells, things and\nfilter are mutually exclusive\". The committed acceptance script could not run as\nwritten. A zone's footprint is always cells, so `zone` now resolves with\n`filterSelectsTargets:false` and reads `filter` itself. Verified after the fix:\none call gives 36 cells + the three medicine defs + Preferred. Regression-checked\nthat `forbid --filter {category:meds}` still resolves `group:Medicine` (4 targets).\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DEMONSTRATE\n\n- 15 of the 26 designator types have no staged target: mine-vein, extract-tree,\n  extract-skull, eject-fuel, fill-in, deconstruct-conduit, release-to-wild, open,\n  strip, slaughter, hunt, uninstall, smooth x3, remove-floor, remove-foundation,\n  claim. They share the engine that chop, mine, tame, deconstruct and forbid\n  exercised, but that is inference, not evidence. `claim` DESERVES A LOOK BEFORE\n  IT IS TRUSTED — the worker flagged that its DesignateThing acts IMMEDIATELY\n  (`SetFaction`) rather than leaving a designation, so it is the one type whose\n  shape differs from every other.\n- The `removal` taxonomy's `mine` / `deconstruct` / `attack` values. My\n  deconstruct sweep over [89,86,30,30] found only `none` — no mineable rock or\n  deconstructible building in range. 2.6 demonstrated all four values; I did not\n  re-demonstrate them through 3.2's verbs.\n- `area` kinds build-roof / no-roof / ignore-roof / snow-clear / pollution-clear,\n  and `area home add/remove` — the `area` verb ran no live step at all.\n- `zone expand` / `shrink`, and the `allow_sow` / `allow_cut` toggles (the\n  refusal gate and `zone edit --filter` were exercised; the geometry ops were not).\n- `unforbid`, and `forbid` as a real mutation — only its dry-run and its fog and\n  reject paths were driven.\n- A pawn confined to an allowed area: that is 3.4's `assign`, sequenced after both\n  merge, per the area-ownership split recorded on this issue.","files":null}]}